  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "getWithdrawnRoutesLength",
    "file": "PcapPlusPlus/Packet++/src/BgpLayer.cpp",
    "description": "BgpUpdateMessageLayer::getWithdrawnRoutesLength() validates/clamps the attacker-controlled withdrawn-routes length using a minimum UPDATE size of only sizeof(bgp_common_header)+sizeof(uint16_t). A BGP UPDATE always also requires the following 2-byte Total Path Attribute Length field, so the clamp can over-report withdrawn-route data by exactly 2 bytes. In this PoV, setWithdrawnRoutes() trusts that over-reported length and shortens the layer two bytes past the valid withdrawn-routes region, leaving a 21-byte UPDATE instead of the required minimum 23 bytes. setNetworkLayerReachabilityInfo() then computes its insertion offset as the 23-byte fixed UPDATE prefix and passes an index two bytes beyond the raw packet end to Packet::extendLayer(); RawPacket::insertData() evaluates m_RawDataLen-atIndex as -2 (converted to a huge size_t), causing the ASan negative-size-param in memmove.",
    "conditions": "A parsed BGP UPDATE must have a declared withdrawn-routes length larger than the bytes available while its BGP message/layer length is at least 21 bytes; the PoV's final UPDATE declares message length 67 and withdrawn length 0xfc00. getWithdrawnRoutesLength() incorrectly clamps this to 46 rather than reserving the mandatory path-attribute-length field (maximum 44). The fuzz harness reads the routes, invokes setWithdrawnRoutes() with modified data, clears them, and restores the original empty parsed vector, shrinking the final layer to 21 bytes. It then adds a 4-byte NLRI prefix; setNetworkLayerReachabilityInfo() attempts insertion at offset 23, two bytes beyond this final layer/raw packet, triggering the invalid memmove."
  },
